<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Kalmyk Khanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1630 CE – 1771 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A khanate of Oirat Mongols who migrated to the Volga steppe, the westernmost Buddhist state in Europe.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the early 17th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torghut">Torghut</a>, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirat</a> Mongol tribe, under their leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kho-orluk">Kho Orluk</a>, migrated west from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungaria</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakh</a> steppe, reaching the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> by about 1630 and driving out the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nogai-horde">Nogai Horde</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayuka-khan">Ayuka Khan</a> later received the title of khan from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmyks">Kalmyks</a> were pastoral nomads following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>, with monasteries and a code of law based on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-oirat-code">Mongol-Oirat Code</a> of 1640. As allies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> they guarded its southern frontier, fighting the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kazakh-khanate">Kazakh Khanate</a> and Ottoman forces. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayuka-khan">Ayuka Khan</a>, ruling from 1669 to 1724, was the khanate's most powerful leader.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Russian settlement and controls on the steppe pushed many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmyks">Kalmyks</a> to return east. In 1771 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ubashi-khan">Ubashi Khan</a> led some 150,000 across the steppe to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungaria</a>, losing many on the way. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a> abolished the khanate, and those left on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> are the ancestors of today's Kalmyks.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kalmyk-khanate.jpg" alt="Kalmyk Khanate" width="320" height="184"><figcaption>Image: naturalearthdata.com, offered to the Public Domain per Terms of Use · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Kalmyk_Khanate.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1630 CE – 1771 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kho Orluk, Ayuka Khan, Ubashi Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tibetan Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kalmyk (Oirat Mongolian)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>, <a href="/countries/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kalmyk Horde, Torghut Khanate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1700&amp;focus=kalmyk-khanate" aria-label="Kalmyk Khanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5628 224 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kalmyk Khanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6209 768h0M5820 533h0M6487 457h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5948 480h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5948 480h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Russia. <a href="/atlas?year=1700&amp;focus=kalmyk-khanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
